[{:db/id -1
  :receipt/id "2026-08-18-source-path-drift-detector"
  :receipt/date "2026-08-18"
  :source/dataset "receipt"
  :receipt/title "13 of 30 shadow-cljs builds cannot compile, and nothing was saying so"
  :receipt/body
  "ADR-2608181700 closed with an open item: `nothing detects source-path drift.
   It was found by hand in two repos and is presumably in others.` This is that
   detector, and the presumption was right.

   ## The failure it exists for

   `shadow-cljs compile` stops at `The required namespace \"x\" is not
   available`, exit 1, before a single test runs. If nothing runs that build,
   nothing ever says so. kotoba-lang/kotobase-worker-shell had been in this
   state long enough that its test suite had NEVER executed; restoring three
   :source-paths turned it on at 45 tests. It was found only because it was one
   of two repos this session happened to touch.

   ## Result

   198 shadow-cljs.edn in checked-out repos; 30 declare a relative sibling
   source-path, which is where drift lives (a repo's own src moving is caught by
   its own compile; a sibling's is not).

       17  ok
       12  UNRESOLVED — a namespace reachable from the build that no
                        :source-path provides, and that EXISTS in this workspace
        1  MISSING     — a declared :source-path that does not exist
                        (kotobase-browser-worker → ../kotobase-cljc-worker/src;
                         the repo exists, the src/ under it does not)

   **13 of 30.** The same three paths I fixed by hand appear three more times
   each — `org-ietf-deflate`, `kotobase-block-codec`, `kotobase-projection` —
   including a third copy of the identical drift in
   net-kotobase-enterprise-wave2/protocols-worker.

   ## How it decides, and why not by compiling

   Compiling 198 repos is not a check anyone will run. It resolves the closure
   the compiler would: seed from the repo's OWN non-relative source-paths, walk
   requires through the siblings, report what nothing provides. An unresolved
   name is then classified, and the two answers mean very different things:

     in this workspace   → the file exists and the build is simply not wired to
                           it. That is the drift; the report prints the
                           :source-path to add.
     not in it           → almost certainly a maven/npm dependency this script
                           cannot see. Reported, never failed on.

   It walks THROUGH a miss when the workspace has the file, so one run reports
   the whole chain. Without that it reports one link per run and the user
   rediscovers the cascade by hand — which is exactly what it replaces.

   ## Two wrong versions, both worth remembering

   1. **A regex over the file: 41 of 41 repos broken.** It matched regex
      character classes (`A-Za-z0-9`), the symbols inside `:refer` vectors
      (`deftest`), local bindings, and the `java.*` imports in a .cljc file's
      `:clj` branch. A detector with a 100% false-positive rate is worse than
      none, because someone has to read it before ignoring it. Replaced with
      balanced-paren parsing of the ns form that takes only the first symbol of
      each entry and descends reader conditionals for :cljs/:default only.
   2. **Scanning every file in every sibling: 37 of 41 broken.** A library
      carries modules a given build never reaches. shadow-cljs compiles a
      closure, not a directory.

   Two smaller ones, both mine: the candidate filter matched `:output-dir
   \"../appview/…\"` and pulled in ten builds that declare no :source-paths at
   all, and the classifier read raw text so a `:source-paths` mentioned only
   inside a comment counted as an unreadable declaration.

   ## Verified in both directions

   - The three repos fixed by hand today read `ok`
     (kotobase-worker-shell, control-plane/protocols-worker, net-kotobase/cypher).
   - Adding the missing path to kura-conformance made `kotoba.kir` resolve and
     the walk advance to the NEXT missing one — the same cascade seen by hand.
   - kura-conformance's finding checked by hand: it declares ../erasure/src but
     not ../kotoba-kir/src, erasure/kotoba_oracle.cljc requires kotoba.kir, and
     kotoba.kir is a real file at kotoba-kir/src/kotoba/kir.cljc.
   - The MISSING finding checked by hand: ../kotobase-cljc-worker/src really
     does not exist.

   ## Where it lives, and why not a fleet gate

   `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`, per ADR-2608125000. **It cannot be a fleet
   gate**: a gate ships only the target repo's own tree, so a detector that
   reads `orgs/` finds an empty workspace on a node and can never go green —
   the reason `root-permit-index` has been 0 pass / 282 fail. Registered with
   the evidence floor the registry requires (`SCANNED\\t[1-9][0-9]*\\t`), so a
   run that dies early is recorded inconclusive rather than clean.

   ## Not fixed here

   The 13 findings are reported, not repaired. Each names the :source-path to
   add, but adding one changes what a build compiles, and a build that starts
   compiling can start failing — that is a change per repo with its own
   verification, not a sweep."
